In the United States, abortion rights are now being attacked - A powerful documentary explains why
U.S. Senate is currently trying to find out whether one of the nine Supreme Court's members could be chosen by President Donald Trump's nominee, Brett Kavanaugh.
In the Republican Party, many people hope that Kavanaugh's decisive vote could overthrow a decision known as Roe versus Wade, which made abortion legal in the United States in 1973.
Ricki Stern and Annie Sundberg's documentary Reversing Roe - The United States and Abortion Rights explains how the current situation has been achieved over the years.
As incredible as it seems, the topic is also topical in Finland.
Minister for Foreign Affairs Timo Soini (sin) has carried out publicity work against safe abortion and received varying degrees of support from the majority of MPs in Finland.
At the beginning of a powerful documentary, gynecologist Colleen McNicholas, who, due to extortion of the laws, has remained the only abortion doctor in the state of Missouri.
The clinic's armed guard says he'll defend U.S. religious freedom.
News videos are reminiscent of deadly terrorist attacks on clinics.
Let's move to a time when abortion was illegal in the United States.
At that time, a consortium of Lutheran clergymen instructed women to see reliable abortion doctors.
The interviewed pastor's arguments are good, but in fact they aren't even needed, a photo of a woman who died from a self-made abortion is enough.
There were about 5000 deaths in the United States per year.
The legalization of abortions was defended especially by the Republican Party.
As early as 1967, Governor Ronald Reagan signed the Abortic Rights Act in California.
Later, Reagan, in his presidential campaign in 1980, began to evocatively confuse religion with politics in order to attract voters.
His successor ­George Bush the Elder also swirled his abortion strain.
The same goes for Donald Trump.
At the deepest core of the matter, the document reaches in search of the underlying reason for the politicization of an abortion issue.
When religious organizations that advocate segregation were deprived of tax exemption, they became politically active.
Abortion was chosen as the hobbyhorse.
Religion has brought exceptional knots to politics.
At the time of writing in the U.S., we are in a situation in a situation where the president, bragging with his sexual harassment but snatching the voices of the religious right with his abortion, has chosen Kavanaugh as the Supreme Court's nominee, which is now accused by several women of sexual harassment.
Kavanaughugh may have the power to decide on the rights of 164 million American women and girls.
Seven Palestinians were shot by the Israeli army, including two children on the border with large demonstrations
Nearly 200 Palestinians have been killed in six months after Israel fired on demonstrators in the border zone.
On Friday, Israeli soldiers shot seven Palestinians in Gaza's border zone, the Palestinian health ministry said.
According to the Palestinian Ministry of Health, two children are among the dead: 12- and 14-year-old boys.
According to the ministry, 505 people have been wounded.
Of these, 89 were wounded by bullets.
The Israeli army has confirmed that it opened the fire and conducted an airstrike on the border zone when protesters threw stones and explosives at Israeli troops.
The army says it has initiated the use of force so that protesters do not break the border fence or aim for the other side.
At least 191 Palestinians have been killed in demonstrations in Gaza since March, when Palestinians started weekly demonstrations at the border.
According to the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights, there are 12 000 wounded.
Demonstrators demanded access for Palestinian families in areas where Palestinians were forced to leave when the State of Israel was founded in 1948.
A Palestinian sniper has killed an Israeli soldier.
Explosives that have been dropped on Palestinian kites and helium balloons have ignited forest areas and farmland in Israel.
