"I recorded The Divinyls' worldwide hit to remind women to regularly do self-attaching [breast's] checks," Williams wrote in an Instagram post.
And yes, this put me in a zone of discomfort, but I wanted to do it, because the subject touches all women, regardless of skin color, anywhere in the world.
Early observation is everything - it saves many lives.
Chrissy Amphlett, who died of breast cancer five years ago at the age of 53.
The music video is part of the I Touch Myself project, which has been created in honor of Chrissy Amphlett.
"She passed away from breast cancer but gave us a hit song to remind women to put their health first," Williams said.
The video has now been viewed 1.5 million times.
It was released early Sunday morning.
In the comments of the video, Williams is praised for his courage.
Earlier this week, Williams opted out of the Beijing tennis tournament.
There's a controversy at the US Open three weeks ago.
Williams fired the head referee of the match and argued, among other things, that men are accepted for much more violent behavior than female players.
