Is it worth going to work tomorrow with a small cold?
Here's the facts for and against
The flu season is hot.
A lot of people want to stay at home or not
However, it's a good idea to think about whether it's a good idea for others to get infected, as the disease can be contagious, even if your own symptoms are small.
Sometimes the symptoms of the flu suddenly hit, sometimes they appear in the sneaks.
In the latter option, you may already infect others, even if your own symptoms seem small, according to the Prevention publication.
Is it worth staying home?
According to Prevention, the flu is contagious even when the person has no symptoms.
The virus is transmitted the day before the symptoms start and from then on from five days to a week.
Children may infect the disease longer.
As soon as you recognize the symptoms, you shouldn't be at school or at work.
You can go back to work when your cough eases and there's no fever anymore, says Gregory Poland, a professor of medicine.
Training should also be avoided.
A too quick return to the gym slows down recovery.
In addition, remember to wash your hands, squeeze into a handkerchief and throw the snot paper in the trash.
Hurry to move, though?
If you have to leave your home when you're sick, consider wearing a protective mask.
The mask doesn't control the viruses, but keeps the user aware of the area of the head.
That's when you don't put your fingers in your nose or mouth, Poland says.
Sneezing is also limited with the mask.
How to avoid infections?
The best way is still to wash your hands and avoid touching your face.
However, it is difficult to avoid the latter.
For example, in the United States, the average person touches their eyes, nose or mouth every 15-30 seconds, Poland warns.
