Restaurant ship M/S Tampere ravaged in Pyhäjärvi - "Sturdyly caught on the rocks"
Inland waterway ship M/S Tampere ran aground late on Friday evening in Akaka in Pyhäjärvi.
The rescue department was alerted to a water traffic accident on Friday night after midnight.
The 30-metre vessel M/S Tampere has drifted into the rocks and is firmly attached to its bow on the rocks, said on-call Fire Chief Matti Willman.
There were 19 passengers and five crew members on board.
According to the emergency services, personal injuries were avoided.
Passengers and almost all crew members have been evacuated to the mainland away from the ship.
Now they are being transported by bus towards Tampere," Jari Nieminen, the on-call manager, told STT after one o'clock at night.
Ambulances were alerted to the port to receive evacuated passengers.
For some reason, a ship that had travelled from Hämeenlinna to Tampere had drifted off its route to the west of Jumusensalmi.
The police are investigating the cause of the events.
Earlier in the evening, on-call Fire Chief Matti Willman told that the conditions on the lake are difficult due to the darkness and the rather strong breeze.
According to the rescue department, the fate of the ship is unclear.
Apparently, the ship doesn't bleed.
After the evacuation operation, the divers begin to inspect the situation at the bottom.
Built in 1957, M/S Tampere is now an inland waterway vessel operating as a restaurant ship with a maximum permissible number of 180 passengers.
There is also a meeting room for private use on board.
According to the ship's website, the cruiser has been restored in 2001, 2014 and 2017.
