Ex-managers demand unpaid fees from Robert Helenius
Boxer Robert Helenius' night's fight against Erkan Teper overshadows the money dispute.
Ilta-Sanomat today reported on a summons filed by boxing manager Jaakko Helminen and deputy referee Jussi Kokkila, in which Helenius is charged a total of just over 80 000 euros in unpaid management fees.
According to the subpoena application, Helminen and Kokkila worked for Helenius 15.4.2014-30.1.2015.
We've been trying to reconcile, but there's no other option.
There has been a clear assignment here, and a lot of work has been done," Kokkila tells Ilta-Sanomat.
Helenius' current manager, Markus Sundman, sent Iltalehti a reply informinging the boxer's view of the events.
Helenius denies being obliged to pay the required amount.
According to Vastinen, Kokkila and Helminen had provided their services financially in an awkward situation to Helenius and promised that they would be able to arrange matches and sponsorship income for him.
It was verbally agreed that Helenius would be obliged to pay only percentages of his matches.
It was recurringly assured that Helenius does not have to pay anything, only to receive the proceeds arranged by them.
This is also a normal and the only practice in the management activities of professional athletes.
There has never been an agreement on paid work, and it would also have been irrational in Helenius's situation, Sundman explains in a message he wrote.
There was never a written agreement on cooperation.
According to the response, Helenius would have wanted to agree on the distribution of income in writing from the beginning, but Kokkila and Sundman did not produce the draft until the end of 2014.
He stated that the proposal was poor on his own behalf and interrupted cooperation with Kokkila and Helminen.
Helenius did not once in contact with Kokkila and Helminen.
A year after the end of the cooperation, Helenius had been assigned an "unindivisible but large-scale" invoice for the manager's work.
This year, the same claims have been filed.
Helenius considers Helminen's and Kokkila's retrospective activities to be cowardly, the reply states.
Helenius will today face Erkan Teperi, who is seventh in the IBF's heavy-duty challenger list in Stuttgart.
A heartfelt man and a good journalist
Entertainment artist and music producer Ilkka "Ile" Vainio knew the deceased radio presenter and journalist Erkki Pälli for decades.
Erkki Pälli, a long-time radio presenter and journalist, died at the age of 83 from incurable liver cancer.
The presenter's obituary was published in Sunday's Helsingin Sanomat.
According to the announcement, Pälli died on 3 September.
Pälli told about the serious illness on his Facebook page in March 2018.
At that time, the publication was also seen by Ile Vainio, who was saddened by the acquaintance's fate.
He already said goodbye on Facebook earlier, Ile Vainio says.
Vainio considers Erkki Pälli to be a multi-talented expert who turned out to be a great journalist.
He recalls that Pälli was a well-liked person with fast whistles and funny things.
I got to know Erkki as a little boy, when I first visited Finnlevy when I was 12 years old and started there as a warehouse man.
That's when Erkki and I met.
At the time, he was Finnlevy's spokesperson and in various positions, Ile Vainio recalls.
As I progressed to make albums as a music producer, there were hundreds of occasions where Erkki Pälli was a regular reporter at all the parties to write stories about artists.
He was always knowledgeable and I could see what a great fire he had for entertainment, music and people.
When Erkki interviewed someone, he got directly into the skin of the interviewees.
He was a heartfelt man. He was a good journalist.
For many, he became known as a well-respected radio presenter, but he also wrote great newspaper articles.
Erkki interviewed me many times.
He was good to talk, Vainio praises.
Over the decades, Pälli worked not only for journalistic work, but also at the Fazer record company Toivo Kärki.
He worked as a music producer on Vesa-Matti Loiri's albums.
Pälli's greatest passion, however, was radio work, in which he had a distinctive style.
He has been doing radio work since the 1960's, and he became familiar to numerous listeners in Yle's ethere.
He retired in 1998.
