The careful planning and financial management of our operations has collapsed throughout our entire organization, Kulmala says in the press release.
We have also succeeded well in partner acquisition, and in addition, the World Cup production was the maximum during the past competition season.
However, in the future, cost management must also be accurate, and at the same time, we must constantly look for new financing models.
I heard my wife scream for help, but then there was silence
Dozens of bodies are lying in the hospital's backyard in the town of Palu on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.
Only one building separates them from the area where injured survivors have been assembled for the assessment of the need for injuries and treatment.
Patients are also treated outdoors due to aftershocks.
One of those in the hospital area was the 52-year-old Baharuddin, who searched for his son.
Like many Indonesians, he uses only one name.
I've got one kid, and he's missing.
“The last time I spoke to him was when he was going to school,” Baharuddin told AFP.
Dwi Haris, who has been injured on his back and his arm, was lying in the courtyard of the Army's hospital.
At the start of the quake, he was at a local hotel with his wife and daughter.
The family was invited to Paluu's wedding party.
I'd probably be squeezed under a collapsed wall.
I heard my wife scream for help, but then there's silence.
I don't know what's happened to him and my child.
“I hope they’re safe,” Haris told the AP news agency, according to British media.
Small shops are being looted
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother of a small baby, tried to reassure her febrile baby.
He described the vibes in the city as intense.
Clean water is scarce.
Small shops are robbed everywhere, Kusuma said.
Many residents of Palu have slept outdoors or in temporary bamboo huts.
Even if the house is somehow standing, possible aftershocks could destroy it once and for all and burieve the residents.
According to news agency, more than half of Palu's prisoners escaped after the prison walls collapsed.
There were reportedly about 560 prisoners, so there may be about 300 fugitives.
Some of Donggala's jails have also escaped, according to The Jakarta Post.
According to the newspaper, the prisoners enraged and set the prison on fire after the guards had forbidden them to leave to look for relatives affected by the earthquake and tsunami.
Palu Airport has been reopened mainly for assistance flights.
Among the first, an army C-130 transport plane landed at the airport, which imported relief supplies.
Only a limited number of commercial flights are allowed at Palu Airport.
Pilots will have to land at the airport without the help of an air traffic control, as the air traffic control tower was badly damaged by an earthquake.
