Sari Piippo left her career in Brussels and became a stay-at-home mother in Finland - the idea of a comics came to mind: the lack of drawing skills didn't slow down
At the time, organic nonsense started from my husband's statement, in which he proposed it as a person to the story I had developed, says Sari Piippo, a comic book blogger, a housewife and former EU translator from Espoo.
Sari Piippo says that she has been in Brussels as an EU translator in financial and legal matters until, after her fourth child, the family became a returnee ten years ago.
I've had a great time as a stay-at-home mom.
This has been the best time of my life, but at some point it came to my mind, what would I do when I grew up, when even the smallest child was starting to be big.
And then, in 2014, there's a cartoon.
Piippo says that he had never dreamed of making a comic, even though he had been reading Donald Duck, Tintin and Asterix since he was little.
The last time I had drawn as a child, but when these manuscripts began to emerge, the situation changed.
At first, I wrote dozens of manuscripts for the desk drawer.
My daughter Viivi Valkama (b. 2000) is a skilled artist, but I couldn't lure her along until the summer of 2016, when I bribed Viivi with the help of a drawing board.
His enthusiasm lasted for a dozen strips, and then I had to start trying for myself, Piippo laughs, who says that the drawing board was a great supporter for him.
The drawing board is an easy and convenient tool to try to get something done.
I didn't even try to draw on paper, says Piippo about his relationship with drawing, which he usually does in the evenings.
The manuscripts come to mind all the time, but usually I don't finish it at once.
It takes up to several hours for one three-screen strip.
May make a screen at a time and I'm happy to play with colors.
Starting drawing is always the hardestiest, because drawing isn't my strongest sport.
Sari Piippo says that she appreciates, for example, Scott Adams' Dilbert from contemporary newspaper comics.
The characters of organic nonsense are a carrot-bodied mother with a salad hair.
Other characters are Mies, Pissis and Kaksonet.
In the world of organic nonsense, man is what he eats.
The ideas of the series come from your own life and social media.
Sari Piippo doesn't want to preach or offer a compulsive bun, even if something sometimes sticks in the organic nonsense.
Piippo says that he reads his series with his husband and children.
It's such a great feeling when you look at the cartoon you're drawing and saying that I've done this today.
There are plenty of challenges for a housewife!
You can read the ‘Organism’ series online at organichomppa.sarjakuvablogit.com
