Indonesian Earthquake: Air Traffic Controller Sacrificed Himself Didn't Leave A Cracked Tower Until Hundreds of Passengers Were Protected
Anthonius Gunawan Agung, a 21-year-old air traffic controller, has emerged as the hero of the Indonesian earthquake.
Agung was left alone in the air traffic control tower until he was able to safely escort a passenger plane carrying hundreds of people from an earthquake-shaken island.
The youngster was in air traffic control at Mutiara SIS Al-Jufrie in the town of Palu when an earthquake struck on Friday.
As the others escaped from the air traffic control tower, Agung refused to leave his position before being able to disploy Batik Air's passenger plane from the runway into the air.
In this way, he could save hundreds of lives, the Indonesian Federal Aviation Administration told AFP.
Mutiara SIS Al-Jufrie airport on Saturday after the earthquake.
The air traffic control tower trembled and cracked.
In the end, Agung tried to save by jumping from the fourth floor of the tower.
His leg was fractured and he suffered serious internal organ injuries.
Agung was rushed to a nearby hospital.
He was to be transported by helicopter to a better equipped facility, but he died 20 minutes before the helicopter arrived, a spokesman for the aviation administration told The Jakarta Post.
The quake continued to intensify when the flight got into the air, and its magnitude was eventually measured at 7.5 magnitudes.
At least 832 people lost their lives.
