Rehn pondered on Yle's Europe's end to the trade war: "You have to watch the next tweet"
The U.S.-China trade war is reflected in the global economy as a whole.
Growth forecasts have been lowered everywhere in Europe, and the Bank of Finland has also slightly lowered its own economic growth estimates.
Olli Rehn, Director-General, said on Yle's 1st morning that even though tightening tension has no direct impact on Europe's trade flows, it undermines confidence, which in turn is reflected in companies' investment decisions.
The situation also applies to Finnish companies' exports.
Rehn thinks Europe is very talented so far.
It has been able to build an interim peace with the United States and has also calmed the situation with China and Japan.
According to Rehn, Europe doesn't seem to be grinding into a trade war.
We'll have to look at the next tweet and then evaluate it.
“We have an unpredictable partner in the direction of Washington,” he said in an interview with Yle.
There are also risks in Europe.
One of them is the third-largest national economy in the euro area, Italy, which is also one of the most indebted countries in the euro area and in the Western world.
The market reacted drastically yesterday to the Italian government's clear deficit budget on Thursday evening.
Next year's deficit will be more than 2.4 per cent of GDP.
Rehn finds the situation in Italy worrying.
Five per cent of the country's loan stock has problem loans and more than ten per cent of non-organised loans.
In addition, its public finances are weak and the country seems to once again exceed its commitment in relation to its European partners.
It may not be a good idea to paint new crises yet, Rehn replied when he was asked whether Italy would take Europe into a new economic crisis.
Another issue affecting Europe is Britain's withdrawal from the EU.
Rehn hoped it would be in harmony.
Conflicted separation would have much more negative effects.
Rehn started as Governor of the Bank of Finland in July.
Mario Draghi, President of the European Central Bank (ECB), will step down in just over a year.
The discussion of his successor has begun.
The name of both Rehn and his predecessor Erkki Liikanen glimpsed in the speeches.
It probably doesn't matter if Finnish names are included, Rehn said and added that he doesn't intend to comment on the matter any further than this.
