What is a good leader like?
There has been a lot of talk about leadership in Finland this week.
Risto Siilasmaa, Chairman of Nokia's Boarding, spoke in an interview with HS about his book Paranoid Optimist, in which he sheds light on his philosophy of leadership.
At the same time, he criticizes the leadership of his predecessor Jorma Ollila.
Ollila strongly denied the accusations, but previous Nokia reports have also talked about the inability of the leaders to act as a team; the fear and silence, which partly caused the collapse of the mobile phone business.
The company's rising risk-taking ability and agility disappeared.
During the week, the Nordic Business Forum was also organised in Helsinki, which drew the cream of Finnish business life to Messukeskus.
In addition to Barack Obama, the stars of the business world took to the stage.
There was enough talk of good leadership.
Gary Hamel, a legend of leadership studies, said that a good leader should have the courage to take the company to the future even when the company is doing well.
The problem is that decisions are made by those who are mentally most tied to the decisions of the past.
John Mackey, the founder of Whole Foods, the founder of the Whole Foods store chain, said that the manager must be able to communicate to the entire staff the purpose of the company.
And that's not just about making a profit for the owners.
Susan Cain, the author of an introvert book about introverts, said that the most successful leaders have been studied.
They act calmly, quietly and decisively.
Often they are introverts.
Aswath Damodaran, a professor of finance, pointed out that the qualities of a good manager must coincide with the stage of the company's life cycle.
In the early stages of the company, the manager must be able to tell the story on which the future of the company is based.
Growing into a mature stage, the company needs a manager who knows the numbers.
These lessons were listened to at the Nordic Business Forum by 7,500 visitors - mainly Finnish leaders.
They were in danger of being overwhelmed with information.
It takes a lot for a good leader.
That's why leadership so often goes wrong, even at the highest level.
Siilasmaa and Ollila were also present.
They talked to each other behind the scenes comradely.
However, they hardly wondered if they were the right leaders for Nokia at the right time.
And it wasn't easy for an outsider to judge their humility.
