Macedonia's name has been divisive for decades - now the people are voting on the matter in disputed elections
Macedonia, based in the Balkans, is currently voting on a proposal for a new name.
According to the agreement between the Macedonian and Greek governments, the Republic of North Macedonia would be named.
Until now, Greece has vetoed Macedonia's attempts to become members of NATO and the EU.
According to Greece, the name Macedonia belongs to its northern province.
The question of the referendum does not mention a new name, but asks whether the voter is in favour of Macedonia's EU and NATO membership by accepting the agreement.
Voting began sluggishly, at least at a school in the center of the capital Skopje.
Retired nurse Olivera Argirosvka, who voted at the school, hoped for support for the agreement.
A referendum will mean a change if it opens the doors to Europe and NATO.
It means a change, especially for young people, he said.
In 1991, an independent Macedonia was officially referred to as the Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia.
Prime Minister Zoran Zaevi's government has campaigned for the adoption of the agreement.
The nationalist right-wing opposition, including President Gjorge Ivanov, has called for a boycott of the referendum.
In addition to the result, there is plenty to be nervous about whether the turnout will rise above the required 50 per cent.
The referendum is not binding, but the victory of yes votes would allow the mandate to change the constitution.
A two-thirds majority in Parliament is needed for the constitutional amendment.
The Greek Parliament still needs to approve the agreement.
Voting venues close at eight o'clock in Finnish time.
