Canada is battling time to reconcile Nafta with the U.S
Time threatens to run out in negotiations between the United States and Canada on the free trade agreement on Nafta.
The United States has set a deadline for Sunday, and negotiators have been discussing closely throughout the weekend.
Canada's chief negotiator, Foreign Minister Chrystia Freeland, postponed his speech at the UN General Assembly to focus on trade talks.
At the request of President Donald Trump, the United States, Canada and Mexico began negotiations about a year ago to renew the North American free trade agreement Nafta.
At the end of August, Mexico and the United States reached an agreement, but no agreement has been reached with Canada.
The United States and Mexico want to enter into force the agreement before Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador, who won the Mexican presidential election, takes office in early December.
Trump has said that Canada's involvement in the agreement is not necessary.
He has previously informed Congress that plans to sign the agreement at the end of November - whether Canada is involved or not.
As a result of these timetables, the U.S.-Canada agreement text should be ready on Sunday in order to give the U.S. Congression enough time to assess it.
