Students play a rare delicacy: the tragedy of antiquity - the reduced Oresteia has its visuals
Ancient tragedies are a rare delicacy on Finnish stages.
For example, the Finnish National Theatreum last saw the tragedy of antiquity, Sophocleen Filoktetes, in 1988.
Now the Theatre Academy brought the only play trilogy that has survived from the National Theatre Theatre, Aiskhylos Oresteia.
In the Lavat auki! projects, which started in 2015, the acting students of the Theatre Academy work together with a professional director internationally.
This fourth project, as well as the first one, is directed by Mikko Roiha, who lives in Berlin.
Oresteia premiered in Berlin in April.
Roiha's adaptation of Oresteia summarizes the story of the end of the vegetation of the doomed Atreus family and the birth of the rule of law to twenty minutes, especially by condensing choir sections.
The work manages to be very faithful to Kirsti Simonsuure's great translation (2003).
Dramatic lights and a display revealing the sides of the theatrical space emphasize simple and bright stage thinking.
At the center of it is the dynamics between the performers and the choir, who move away from the choir.
The performance uses both double and triple interpretations of name roles.
The actors will not leave the stage at all, so, for example, the murders of Agamemnon, who will return from the Trojan War and his spoils of war, Kassandra, will also be shown on the stage, contrary to what was customary in the ancient performance tradition.
Of course, the performance does not emphasize that it is faithful to the ancient presentation tradition.
Rather, a modern interpretation faithful to the text is created in front of the viewer's eyes, which is constantly aware of its ritual roots.
Roiha has managed to bring out a rhythmic group of master's acting students as a close-knit group, as one of the organisms.
Particularly in choir sections, actors flexibly bent on very expressive, sculpture-like group formations come into their own on the bare stage, decorated only by a white curtain.
Impressive.
Oresteia's performance tour will continue to Kouvola and Oulu Theatre.
