The safety of store aunts and uncles must be ensured
For the first time, I received a salary for a newspaper article in 1984.
When you're amazed by a topic with these annual rings, it's usually big news.
When I wrote (Karjalainen, 27.9.) about the insecurity of retail employees, I was amazed and even a little angry at times.
I also talked to Erika Kähärä, Work Environment Specialist at the Finnish Service Sectors' Union (PAM).
He says the crime stats don't tell the real situation.
The problem is growing, although employers claim otherwise, of course.
Only cases that have been perceived as clearly serious will go to the attention of the police, and many real violent situations will remain unreported.
"Of course, companies are obligated to compile statistics on the basis of the Occupational Safety Act based on their near-miss situation, but these statistics remain for internal use and their data is not collected anywhere," he said.
Mental injuries don't end up in statistics.
In situations of violence, it has always been the problem that they do not always cause physical injuries.
Psychiatric traumas, despite this, can be much more severe than bruises and wounds.
It is difficult to draw the distinction between the violent situation and the imminent situation of violence, when aggressive threats of a certain level must also be counted as violence - even if there is no attack.
Too many people are afraid at their workplace, even if the actual action does not take place.
Some neighborhoods are restless.
The store has acquired guards.
As the opening hours of the stores were liberated, there was also a stagger in the media about its impact on the safety of the vendors.
"At that time, quite a few employers provided guard services at least for nocturnal hours and to prevent working alone," Kähärä commented.
When nothing happens, we draw the wrong conclusion.
This now seems to have had an effective effect, but this will produce a new negative trend.
When, due to security, the disturbances remained well under control, the impression was born that security would be unnecessary.
Now in many places we are trying to find solutions that could eliminate expensive security guard services.
Mild assault is a plaintiff's offence, and it is quite understandable that a seller who has been assaulted does not want to make a request for an investigation into a criminal who usually lives near the store.
However, according to the same point of the law laid down in 2011, mild assault will become subject to the public prosecutor if it occurs at the workplace.
I think we now need precedents.
The next time that the aunt or uncle of the store is assaulted, the police should be investigated and the prosecutor accused.
When the court puts a few abusers in jail, and the verdict from the hard end of the penalty scale, the manners would clean up.
A society in which a grocery store must be open almost day and night must ensure the safety of the store's employees.
