In a clock transfer conversation, the foxholes are deep
A few decades ago, I remember when a magazine asked different Finnish influencers what divides the Finnish people.
Juice Leskinen's answer was the only one in mind: the Nen extension.
Somen's surname has a nen-ending and some don't.
Dividing lines can be created anywhere, and there was one offering here.
In the EU, Finland has achieved the abandonment of annual clock shifting.
Very many people agree that changing clocks twice a year has been the work of fools and caused a wide range of problems.
In the end, this achievement seems to be the easiest phase of the solution, at least in Finland, even though it hasn't been easy.
The hardestiest thing now is to figure out what time of the day will end up: the current summer or winter time or some other time zone.
You can also tell your opinion about this on the otakantaa.fi website.
The survey was opened by the Ministry of Transport and Communications and is open until Friday 12 October.
The discussion takes place intensely at the coffee tables and on social media, and everyone has something to say.
Those who defend the winter time justify their stance with the influence of morning light on people's well-being, and summer time defends people's desire to go outdoors and, consequently, well-being.
Both positions have strong supporters and good arguments.
According to studies, morning light rhythmizes a person's day and gives their vitality.
On the other hand, if you don't particularly suffer from waking up in the dark, the brightness will increase people's movement outdoors and provide better opportunities, for example, for autumn yard work.
The amount of additional light after a normal working day has already been counted to its class.
I'd rather take both, but we can't do anything about our evil location in the northern corner of the earth.
The winters are too dark and the summers are too bright for many anyway.
It is typical for the discussion on the subject that one's own vision is advocated and the other's view is strongly opposed.
You don't really listen to your opponent's arguments, because the most important thing is how you feel.
Since almost everyone has an opinion on the matter, it's somewhat pointless to try to turn another's head.
That's a waste of gunpowder.
The topic is terribly easy to debate, because the matter does not require a great deal of dedication, and a mutu feeling is enough to justify it.
Similar issues are usually disputed the sharpest and equally fruitless.
I'm sorry if the wrong option is chosen, but let's adapt to that, too.
And it's always offering a suitable scapegoat if life feels sad.
