Eno's church on Sunday's 200th anniversary celebrations
The 200-year anniversary celebrations of Eno's church will be celebrated on Sunday in a partly sad atmosphere.
Arson of Kiihtelysvaara church a week ago was a shock.
Eno's church was guarded two nights after the fire.
Despite the pain, today's still a place to celebrate.
Even if that's what no one would want, Eno's church is now the oldest church in the Joensuu Parish Union.
A church on a hill in the middle of the village has always been Eno's heart, regardless of all the municipal and other joints in the world.
The family mass will start at Eno Church today at 10 a.m.
After that, we move to the parish hall at the Karelian banquet table.
There's all sorts of things inside and out throughout the day.
For example, you can get acquainted in the church building under the guidance of Ari Räsänen, who has been a retired parish master for 37 years.
The celebration of the birth of the church also marks the end of one era.
Already decommissioned, the parish hall, built in 1982, will keep its doors open for the last time today.
Now we are in temporary facilities at the former town hall, says chaplain Markku Koistinen.
The parish house was destroyed by indoor air problems, but the church has been standing healthy for 200 years.
Elon Musk recedes from the position of Chairman of Tesla's Board of Director - Fraud charges agreed for US$40 million
Tesla, the company's chief executive Elon Musk, has filed a lawsuit with the U.S. Securities and Markets Authority (SEC).
The SEC suspected Musk was guilty of securities fraud, and Musk was in danger of being sidelined by the company he founded.
However, Musk is allowed to continue as Tesla's CEO.
Musk, on the other hand, is forced to step down as chairman of Tesla's board of directors for three years.
The company will have to hire an external chairman of the board.
Musk is also subjected to stricter controls.
According to the agreement, Tesla will have to hire two independent executives and monitor Musk's communications more closely.
In addition, Tesla and Musk will each pay US$20 million (approximately 17 million euros) to mediate the case.
"The decision is intended to prevent new market disturbances and inconvenience to Tesla's shareholders," said Steven Peikin, president of the SEC.
The incident began in August when Musk tweeted that he might withdraw the company from the stock market.
Musk also wrote that the company's financing is secured.
The tweet briefly increased Tesla's share price.
The SEC suspected Musk was guilty of securities fraud.
According to the agency, the tweet was false and misleading, and Musk hadn't talked about the arrangement with the company's other management or potential financiers.
In practice, the agency accused Muski of misleading investors.
The SEC announced on Friday that it would sue the company and Musk.
The SEC said it demanded that Musk be sentenced to fines and he would no longer be banned from the management of listed companies.
The SEC's challenge frightened investors, and Tesla's share fell sharply.
More than 800 people killed in Indonesia quake and tsunami
The number of deaths in the Indonesian earthquake and subsequent tsunami has risen to at least 832, according to AFP.
The number of casualties is expected to increase further, as the destruction is ongoing.
According to Vice President Jusuf Kalla, the final figure could reach thousands.
Friday's earthquake sparked a tsunami that took place in the city of Palu on the island of Sulawes.
Immediately after the quake, a warning was issued for the tsunami, which, however, was quickly withdrawn.
According to the authorities, almost all of the known deaths are, at least for the time being.
Rescue authorities are concerned, among other things, about the Donggala region near the center of the quake, where the situation is still largely unclear.
Many people are already known to have died.
Requestants for help from the ruins
The Indonesian army has been alerted to help rescue workers look for victims.
President Joko Widodon, who arrived in the area of destruction today, urged the soldiers to carry out rescue work tirelessly day and night.
One of the focal points of the rescue work is a hotel located in Palu, where 50-60 people are still believed to exist in the ruins.
Muhammad Syaugi, the head of the rescue agency, said that one person was able to excavate one person alive from the ruins of the Roa-Roa Hotel, which is popular among business travellers.
We also heard people's requests for help," he said.
According to Syaugi, heavy clearing equipment would quickly be needed for the quake and tsunami area.
Many Indonesians have turned to Facebook and other social media to find their missing loved ones.
Help, you in Palu and nearby.
My friend's family members are still missing," one of the writers pleaded in the Facebook group.
Politician phone numbers leaked into the hands of troublemakers in Britain
Many British politicians were harassed when their contact details were leaked due to deficiencies in app encryption.
Among other things, troublemakers had access to ministers' phone numbers.
It was an application used by the British Conservative Party Congress.
For example, Environment, Food and Rural Affairs Minister Michael Gove and former Foreign Secretary Boris Johnson were subjected to harassment.
A number of MPs received harassment calls.
Due to the lack of encryption of the app, the audience was able to access the profiles of politicians who were in the Conservative Party Annual Meeting's mobile app.
The audience had access to the profiles using a politician's e-mail address, which was readily available online.
The person logging in to the profile was able to view and edit the material stored in the profile.
For a moment, someone managed to change Boris Johnson's profile picture into a porn photo and the title sluggish.
Michael Goven's profile picture was exchanged a picture of media mogul Rupert Murdoch.
Dawn Foster, a columnist for The Guardian, described the issue by typing that the meeting app allows you to log in to other people's profiles with their email addresses.
You can then view the contact information of the profile person and, for example, send messages in their name.
The meeting app made every reporter's, a politician's and participant's phone number public, Foster criticized.
Conservative Party spokesman apologised for the leak.
He said that the technical problems have been solved and the application is now working safely.
The British Information Department (ICO) supports the data leak.
The application was created by Crown Comms, an Australian company.
The Opposition Workers' Party criticises the Conservative Party for not being able to rely on security.
Every week there are a large group of Poles clapping in the Audience of The Wall Finland quiz
Observent viewers paid attention to the reactions of the studio audience of The Wall Suomi.
The Wall, which has spread rapidly around the world, landed as a Finnish version to Nelonen last Saturday.
Hosted by Heikki Paasonen, the game show was filmed in Poland in the same studio, where other international productions of the format are made.
In Poland's studio, the filming possibilities were exceptional, as the large 12-meter-high gaming wall in the format demanded space from the farm.
During the kick-off period, the show's studio audience was brought up on social media.
On Twitter, there was a debate about whether the Poles sitting in the studio might understand anything about a game played in Finnish.
Unne Sormunen, the head of Nelonen's domestic programmes, illuminates the matter to Iltalehti.
He says that the studio audience consists of the players' loved ones, other Finns, expatriate Finns living in Poland and just Polish spectators.
The audience consists of international applauders in Finland's production, just like the audiences of The Wall episodes filmed in the same production space in other countries.
There are also such groups in Finland, so-called professional audiences, who take part in the descriptions of different programmes, Sormunen explains.
He points out that following the game in itself is not so obscure, even if you don't understand the language you're playing it with.
It is easy to tell the difference between red and green balls falling off the wall.
However, there are interpreters who take care of the translation work.
There are two interpreters who, as a simultaneous interpretation, interpret events for the studio director all the time.
And the studio director then instructs the audience and tells what kind of flap is thrown there, for example, and what the situation is going on, Sormunen says.
He compares the situation to how David Hasselhoff acted in domestic production the other day.
A few years ago, David Hasselhoff was involved in Talent Finland's filming in Finland.
He was constantly interviewed for simultaneous interpretation and he knew everything that was spoken in Finnish during the photo shoot, Sormunen sums up.
The typhoon, which has broken the southern parts of Japan, advances towards the main island - authorities warn of landslides and floods
Authorities also warn of possible landslides and floods.
At least 50 people have been injured in typhoon Trami, but authorities say major injuries have been avoided and there is no information on fatalities so far.
On the southern island of Okinawa, the breeze has been so strong that it has turned cars around.
According to the NHK broadcaster, people have been evacuated to shelters, and electricity has been cut off by more than 300 000 households.
More than a thousand flights have been withdrawn, and bullet trains are at a standstill due to the storm.
The storm is expected to hit near Osaka around noon Finnish time.
The capital, Tokyo, is not directly on the route of the storm, but there will also be strong winds and rain.
Trains have been canceled today in Tokyo.
According to weather scientists, Trami will torment Japan until Monday.
