Sabotage activities by the Russian intelligence services have a long tradition
There is no reason to assume that the Foreign Inquiry of the Russian Federation SVR or military intelligence GRU would have given up this policy.
During the Soviet era, so-called DRG groups specialized in sabotage activities.
The name comes from the Russian words diversionno-razvedyvatelnaja gruppa: sabotage and reconnaissance group.
Representatives of the KGB's Western security service prepared detailed plans for important sabotage targets, according to Vasili Mitrohin (1922-2004).
His information was published in Christopher Andrew's books at the end of the 1990's and the early 2000's
The sites included ports, shipyards, large dams, oil refineries and gas pipeline connections.
In Germany, the KGB purchased uniforms and accessories for DRG groups for the Bundeswehr, railways, gamekeepers and road workers.
This trick was used by Russian paratroopers in Crimea in February 2014: they weared local Ukrainian police rioting gear, established inspection stations from the peninsula on roads leading to Ukraine and isolated Crimea from their motherland.
During the Cold War, agents hid supply kits for sabotage groups in the terrain with mines, explosives, lighters and messaging equipment.
As recently as 1998, a KGB's stash was found in Switzerland, which exploded when authorities sprayed it with a water cannon.
At night, the police caught four drunk drivers in the county
