The true story today on TV: The climber was left to die twice
Beck Weathers wrote a book 18 years ago.
Lefted for Dead: My Journey Home From Everest is based on his own experiences.
Tonight's film is based on them.
Everest will now premiere on Finnish TV.
In May 1996, a devastating blizzard surprised climbers on Mount Everest.
Baltasar Kormákur's guidance for 2015 reflects the struggle for survival, which has been richly handled.
We've made films and documentaries about the tragedy before.
We are also going through the real events in this article.
The world's tallest lining took the lives of eight climbers 22 years ago.
Two of them were expedition leaders: Scott Fischer and Rob Hall.
It's Hall who acted as Weathers' guide.
Weathers' life may have been saved by an eye surgery performed before leaving for Nepal.
As a result of it and air pressure, he lost sight, which is why the conquest of the peak had to be skipped.
Rob Hall moved on and never came back.
However, Weathers' struggle for life and death had only just begun.
As the storm arosed, he got lost and could no longer find his way back to the camp.
When they came looking for the man, they found him in such a bad condition that they didn't believe in survival.
Weathers was left to die.
Miraculously, however, he got on his feet and found the others.
It was thought that Weathers was still dying, and once again he was dumped on his own fate.
It's clear that, contrary to different expectations, Beck Weathers survived.
At some point, even fellow climbers began to believe in the possibility enough to be given help.
It's Beck Weathers is played by Josh Brolin.
Rob Hall is Jason Clarke and Scott Fischer Jake Gyllenhaal.
Other roles include Robin Wright, Keira Knightley and Emily Watson.
