Images and videos: Medican flashes its power in Greece - The sea disappeared and the street turned into a river
A hurricane-type autumn storm swirled from southwest to Greece on Saturday.
Hurricane or Medican has been named Zorba.
The Greek Meteorological Institute has issued a red weather warning to the areas of Attica, Peloponnese, the Ionian Islands, the Cyclades and the Crete.
Pictures taken from the region show a lot of floods across Greece.
A video posted on Twitter shows the sudden appearance of a river between the houses in the city of Kiato in the Korinthia region due to the floods.
The second video shows how the car was transported by floodwaters.
In the southern part of the Peloponnese peninsula, strong winds caused strong seafaring.
The Embassy of Finland in Athens instructs people on their Facebook pages to prepare for a storm in Greece.
The embassy advises you to follow weather reports and instructions from the local authorities.
Hurricane in the Medicarne, i.e. a medicane, brings with it heavy rains and even the risk of flooding.
According to CNN, according to a study published in 2011, medicans appear only once or twice a year worldwide, typically around September-October, when sea temperatures are still high.
The storm that is now blowing left behind floods in Tunisia and Libya before continuing its journey north and towards Europe.
Concerns have also been caused by refugee camps in the islands, such as Lesbos.
Thousands of Syrian and Iraqi refugees living in the camps have limited access to proper protection.
Areas destroyed by wildfires will also remain under the storm in summer around Athens.
There was also a medican in Greece last November.
We don't have to go to the sea right now, but flights are still on schedule - thousands of Finns are currently on holiday on the Greek islands threatened by the autumn storm
Excursions to the sea are cancelled, and even when walking, you should be careful not to leave branches or other loose items in a storm.
Such instructions have been distributed by the guides to more than 3,500 Finns on holiday in Greece through Aurinkomatkat on the weekend.
Instructions will come in handy, as even the Zorbas storm, which will escalate into monsters, is predicted to beat the Greek islands this weekend.
For example, in the vicinity of the island of Crete, the wind speed can rise in gusts up to 45 meters per second.
The wretchedness of the weather is increased by heavy rains.
According to tour organisers, anyone who has travelled through Aurinkomatkat and Tui to Greece from Finland who has provided their telephone numbers has been warned of strong winds by text message.
In the air still the sun and warmth
According to Inka Ikonen, a communications specialist at Aurinkomatkat, the worst has been avoided in the resorts by the beginning of Saturday.
For example, flights have landed and risen according to the normal schedule.
Of course, we're monitoring the situation all the time.
We're ahead of safety.
Tui's on-caller Antonia Schroderus says that their flights have also travelled on schedule.
We are in constant contact with our staff at the site.
At least Crete was still peaceful, he said in the early evening on Saturday.
There are currently more than 1,500 Finnish passengers on the most popular Greek islands, Rodos and Crete.
In addition, a few hundred passengers are vacationing in smaller destinations.
For example, the worst storm on the island of Kosi is expected to reach on Sunday morning.
On the other hand, on Saturday afternoon in Rodos, the sun had glimpsed and the temperature climbed to 27 degrees Celsius, says Ikonen from Aurinkomatkat.
Zorbas is estimated to be the next nuisance of Turkey, albeit slightly mitigated.
We've already been in contact with Turkey and our staff there, and we've checked, for example, the number of Finns who flowed to Antalya," Schroderus says.
