Two robot cars will start operating in Espoo
They will operate for the rest of the year and pick up passengers, the City of Espoo announces.
Otaniemi's robot cars will play the role of a bus simulator, as next spring the area intends to start traffic with a robot bus.
The project develops and pilots robotic vehicle traffic that operates throughout the year and tests the technical functionality of vehicles in Finnish conditions.
The experiment reveals how robot cars fare on the current street network.
At the same time, the city offers robot bus developers the opportunity to develop technology in a real-world environment, says Traffic Management Manager Johanna Nyberg.
For the first time, robot cars run completely automatically among other traffic without special traffic arrangements.
The safety of the vehicles is always the responsibility of the auxiliary passenger on board.
Vehicles sense their surroundings all the time with several different sensors and assess whether the driveway is available.
If it is not, they will interpret whether the detected obstacle is a vehicle, a bicycle or, for example, a snowflake," says Harri Santamala, CEO of Sensible 4, which is responsible for the development of robotic traffic.
Robot cars run the whole autumn three days a week: on Tuesdays at 13-15.30, on Wednesdays at 10-15 and on Thursdays at 10-15.
Robot cars stop at six stops along the route and pick up passengers.
Robotic car stops can be identified by stop markings.
You are most surely able to get a ride from stop number 1 in Otakaari.
