The police supervised hunting in North Karelia and Savo
The Eastern Finland Police Department carried out hunting supervision on Friday and Saturday with Metsähallitus and the Finnish Wilderness Centre in North Karelia and Ylä-Savo, wilderness contact persons of the Police Department reported on social media on Saturday.
Supervision's main focus was on the legality and permissibility of hunting.
Each hunter was inspected with a hunting licence and a hunting permit required on state-owned lanes.
In addition, possession permits for hunting weapons and the permissible quantities of self-loading weapons used in chickering were inspected.
According to the police, 112 hunters were inspected during surveillance, and only one hunter who lacked the required hunting permit was encountered.
The hunter was fined 12 days for illegal hunting.
In addition, 133 drivers of the vehicle were blown by the police, and two of them are suspected of drink-driving.
In addition, patrols dealt with five traffic violations in the fine procedure, the police said.
In conclusion, hunters comply very well with the permissibility of hunting.
Many hunters were surprised by surveillance.
A hunter stated that "I've been hunting in the area for 50 years and this was the first time someone checks the permits."
The Eastern Finland Police Department will continue hunting supervision in the autumn together with other supervisory authorities.
Mykkänen will go to Turkey to monitor the selection of Syrian quota refugees
Minister of the Interior Kai Mykkänen (National Coalition Party) will travel to Turkey in the next few days to monitor the selection of quota refugees coming to Finland.
It has been decided to take 530 Syrian refugees from Turkish refugee camps in Finland this year.
Selection trips are usually made a couple of times a year, accompanied by representatives from the Finnish Immigration Service, the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Employment and the police.
The delegation is currently conducting interviews in Turkey.
The United Nations Refugee Agency (UNHCR) first selects particularly vulnerable people.
In practice, it has meant people who are considered to be particularly distressed in refugee camps, such as single-parent families, people with disabilities and the sick, women and children.
More people will be selected for the group of those coming to Finland than in the quota, because in practice some will not arrive for different reasons.
Mykkänen will also meet Turkish Interior Minister Süleyman Soylu and NGOs on the Syrian border.
Ilkka Salmi, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of the Interior, will also participate.
We've talked about raising the quota
Finland has been taking quota refugees since the 1970's.
In recent years, the quota has been 750 refugees per year.
For a couple of years, the quota was 1,050 per year, but no agreement has since been reached by the Board of Directors.
The National Coalition Party has advocated an increase in the quota, but the project has most recently fallen on the resistance of the blues.
Prime Minister Juha Sipilä (Centre Party) recently proposed gradually increasing the quota to 1 500-2 000 people per year.
Mykkänen's visit is the first during this government term that the Minister of the Interior will make on the site to monitor the selection of quota refugees.
The previous interior ministers Petteri Orpo (National Coalition Party) and Paula Risikko (National Coalition Party) had time to move to their new duties before the visits could be arranged.
Finland receives 10 000 euros from the EU for each particularly vulnerable refugee.
Now let's get lost - you don't know if you're crying or laughing anymore
Last Saturday, Karjalainen announced a bill to amend the Animal Welfare Act.
The article gave the impression that, in principle, the bill prohibits the keeping of all animals that are not specifically permitted by the decrees of ministries in connection with production animals, pets or other business activities.
In other words, everything else is generally prohibited, except the animals mentioned in the regulation separately.
If the bill ends up as a law, it probably means that in Western democracies we are rejecting the general principle that the law specifically prohibits activities that jeopardize society's security or good practice.
As a rule, we are being to enact the prohibition of all activities for which the law or regulation does not separately authorise.
The amendment means that nothing new can be tried, for example, in business operations or do anything else until a law or regulation that approves it has been adopted.
The Government's legislative drafting in general has been weak.
If we now embark on the path that, as a rule, in Finland, everything is forbidden, which is not specifically allowed, you no longer know whether to cry or laugh.
I don't have any doubt that the Animal Welfare Act requires an update.
But a legislative project in this direction is not part of democracy.
Unfortunately, I do not know the Constitution very well, but I wonder if it accepts such a "everything is forbidden, which is not specifically permitted by law or regulation".
I hope to find a sensible minister who will suspend the progress of such a legislative project.
