Sike's apple-pear cake takes your tongue away
This recipe is for those of you who have good, sour apples on their own, as the domestic apple is shockingly expensive in the store.
I might have to buy a pear, even though quite a lot of pear trees are already growing.
This cake can also only be made from apples if you don't want to buy a pear.
Of course, the taste is more versatile, if you also invest in the cake.
Peel the apples and pear and remove the seeds.
Slicing.
Melt the butter in a saucepan.
Whisk the eggs and sugar until foamy, then add the cornflour and melted butter to the foam.
Combine the cornstarch and baking powder and add them to the dough.
Add almond flour and wheat flour, mix well.
Line the bread tin with baking paper, sprinkle 2 tablespoons of sugar on the bottom of the pan and spread the pear slices on top of the sugar.
Spoon half of the dough over the pear slices and the apple wedges on top of the dough.
Spoon the rest of the dough over the apple wedges.
Cook in the oven at 180°C for about an hour until the surface is beautifully browned.
Let cool in the tin, then pour into a serving platter.
Cut the cake properly when cooled.
The cake has a brittle texture and crumbles easily, because the dough contains almond and corn flour without gluten.
Ruka will host an international crime literature event in January
Kuusamon Rukka will host an international literary festival focusing on Nordic crime fiction for the first time in January.
The Nordic Noir programme consists of interviews, panel discussions and other literary-themed programme numbers, as well as jazz music.
Jens Lapidus, the star of Swedish detective literature, Jerker Eriksson and Håkan Axlander Sundquist, the star of Swedish detective literature, will be the literary guests of the festival.
In addition to Swedish names, the event will also feature author guests from Iceland, Norway and Finland.
Finnish writers at the event are represented by Max Seeck.
According to the organizers of the festival, the northern location, nature, the dark season and the unique surroundings of Kuusamo provide a framework for a unique literary experience.
Status symbols don't attract friends - Four facts about popularity
What makes a person popular, charismatic or admirated?
Researchers at the University of Sydney, who analysed this, have outlined two types of "cooliness" consisting of slightly different traits: charming coolness in general and dissident-cooliness that partly contradicts it.
According to the researchers' description, attractively cool people had many features that were considered socially desirable: they were outgoing, friendly, harmonious and competent.
Dissidents, on the other hand, were characterized by distance, independence, rebellion and edge.
They thirsted for the experiences and gave little attention to the assessments of outsiders.
Both types were united by good self-esteem and a stable emotional life.
The role of Kovis in the long run is not cool: high status in adolescence does not necessarily lead to success later in life.
According to a study published in the journal Child Development in 2014.
Young people who dated early, partyed, sought good-looking groups and strived for the popularity of others, had an increased risk of using intoxicants and getting into crimes as young adults.
In their relationships, they fared weaker than their most inconspicuous peers.
Locker cars, brand bags or watches that communicate high status do not make people cool - or at least not genuinely attractive company.
In a study published in the journal Social Psychological and Personality Science in August, the vast majority of people were more prepared to become friends with an ordinary supermarket shirt than those dressed in a branded shirt - despite the fact that most people chose to wear a branded jersey in the event of an introductory.
If you're on the holiday islands of Greece, watch out!
A hurricane is about to be hit there - a weather warning is screaming red
During the weekend in southern Greece, it may rain as much water as in Finland, on average, over a six-month period.
Hurricane Zorbas will hit Greece over the weekend.
Be prepared if you are staying in Peloponnese, Ionian Islands, Cyclades or Crete.
The storm is very strong, so the Embassy of Finland in Athens announces it on its Facebook page.
According to the major embassy, the Greek Meteorological Service has issued a red weather warning to the regions.
The impending storm will also be reported in the weather service Foreca's blog.
According to it, the storm's toughest gusts are up to 45 meters per second.
By comparison, the Kuisma storm, which beat Finland in the early part of the week, blew at its hardest at just over 31 metres per second.
According to the blog, however, the biggest concern of storm Zorbas is not the wind, but the heavy rains.
There could be more than 300 millimetres of water in southern Greece this weekend.
It corresponds to about half of Finland's annual precipitation.
The weather service Severe Weather Europa has shared a video of the development of the storm on its Facebook page.
Zorbas storm has caused floods in Tunisia and Libya earlier this week.
