Erno Kinnunen from Kitee won the Finnish Rally Championship
Rally car championships ended rarely.
Hardly ever before have the brothers ended up as Finnish champions at the same time.
Eerik and Henrik Pietarinen from Tuusula did so in the final rally around Turku and Salo, as Eerik won the SM1 and Henrik SM2 classes.
Erno Kinnunen from Kitee secured the best achievement of his career when the third place in the final race sealed the Finnish championship from the SM4 class of two-wheel drive cars.
Kinnunen and co-driver Ville Mäkelä, who competed at Honda Civic, started the race in Turku in moderation, because the starting points were clear: the ranking in the top group would guarantee the Finnish Championship title.
In the darkness of Friday night, the duo had to give a handicap when some of the additional lights failed on the second special stage.
That's where you got to sift the third snippet quite blindly.
It's not appropriate to relax in a situation like that.
On Saturday, Jussi Teppo, who was only second in the series, was followed, Kinnunen said.
Syria calls on the United States, France and Turkey to withdrew its troops
Syrian Foreign Minister urges the United States, France and Turkey to withdraw their troops from Syria.
Speaking at the UN General Assembly on Saturday, Foreign Minister Walid Muallem said foreign troops must leave Syria immediately and unconditionally.
Muallem said that foreign troops are in Syria fighting terrorism, and the war against terrorists is "almost over."
In his speech to the United Nations, Muallen also appealed to Syrian refugees to return to their home country.
There is no end to the seven-year war.
More than 360 000 people have died in the war.
Chancellor Angela Merkel said on Friday in Germany that a meeting on Syria was planned for October, which would also be attended by the presidents of France, Russia and Turkey.
It's not exactly the Super Bowl, but tens of millions of Americans watched the Supreme Court's judicial drama on TV
Judge Brett Kavanaugh, who is nominated for the Supreme Court, and Christine Blasey Ford, who accuses him of being harassed, got an exceptionally large number of people on the screens.
In the U.S., a full-fledged U.S. Supreme Court's nomination-drama received at least 20 million people in front of their televisions on Thursday.
At the time, Kavanaugh, appointed by Donald Trump, and Blasey Ford, a California professor who accused him of harassment, were in front of the Senate Judiciary Committee to tell his story.
According to the preliminary results, the hearings were followed by a total of 20.4 million viewers on six main channels: ABC, CBS, CNN, FOX News, MSNBC and NBC.
In addition to them, there were still several millions more to hear online.
NBC reported that the live stream was followed by 6.8 million people and CNN reported that it had set a one-year record with 8.2 million live broadcast launches.
CNN's figure rises by more than 10 million, including those who have followed the broadcast via Youtube and Facebook.
Fox News Digital also launched 8.0 million.
Digital start-ups are the channels' own, the TV ratings come from the research and data analysis company Nielsen.
Nielsen will publish the final readings on Monday, but they will be dizzying anyway.
Few can reach 20 million
More than 20 million spectators in the United States are usually accessed only by sporting matches as well as the major Oscar and Grammy galas in the entertainment world, as well as occasional political events such as speeches by presidents, as well as presidential debates in 2016.
Of course, it's time for the U.S.'s most watched program, the U.S. Football League's NFL's Super Bowl.
This year, the Super Bowl gathered about 103,10 million people on NBC.
According to Nielsen, nearly 48 million people and more than 30 million people watched Trump's first speech to congress last year.
That's very important
The Supreme Court's nomination of judges is an extremely significant case in the United States, as the posts are for life.
Trump was able to nominate a new judge when Antonin Scalia passed away in early 2016 and Republicans managed to prevent then-President Barack Obama from appointing a successor.
The second place opened when Judge Anthony Kennedy, 81, announced his retirement this year.
Kennedy was appointed by Republican President Ronald Reagan, but on several issues he was a so-called libra language whose vote could decide the position of a nine-member court.
He was a supporter of the right to abortion and the rights of sexual minorities and, for example, decided with his vote in 2015 that same-sex people were granted the right to marry throughout the federal state.
Kavanaugh is much more conservative.
His election to the Supreme Court may lock the court's Conservative Supremacy for decades.
Children have the right to receive information for parents
Only the parents of children under the age of 10 can see their children's health information in the Kanta service.
It's a big problem, it's wrong and it's strange.
Initially, the situation of the Kanta service was, of course, even more worrying than this: the parents did not get to look at even the health information or recipes of the babies.
A temporary correction to the situation was introduced in the autumn of 2016 for children under the age of 10.
Since then, practices have been promised to be corrected, but it has been surprisingly long.
Now the repair is on the way - in 2020.
Since the problem is clear, it shouldn't have been more to change the legal basis than a shout-out.
After that, with a layman's understanding, it's a matter of pressing the button: now that the wire is closed, let's open it.
Of course, the child can show their information to their parents - if they have bank codes or an official identity card.
But how many 10-year-olds have?
The ID card is subject to a fee, and the 10-year-old doesn't even get bank credentials.
In practice, therefore, no one can see the information of the elementary school's fourth grader other than by asking them printed.
It is not so much about the right of the parents as the right of the children.
It is of the utmost importance for the care of the child that the parents can be involved in it.
Of course, the right of teenagers to privacy must be ensured.
Minors can also have family situations in which all things cannot be freely talked about.
Often they are related to sexual health, of which free care is a deeply meaningful human right.
