Man threatens to kill cops at a police station in Kajaani
The captured brawler continued to bulge even in the chamber.
Police say they put the man in a police jail to "calm down."
The police were the subject of a suspected assaulter on Saturday night in Kajaani.
The burglary announces a case that started when a 30-year-old man fiercely challenged a quarrel in a central restaurant.
The man first assaulted another client inside the restaurant.
As a result, removal from the restaurant.
Outside, a fighter attacked another man by knocking him to the ground.
When a third party tried to intervene, the man hit him, too.
The triple assault led to a police call.
The patrol apprehended the man and took him to the Kajaani police station, police said.
The suspect still behaved aggressively at the police station.
He kicked another officer in the leg.
He threatened to kill and assault the cops.
Police say they sent the man to a police jail to "calm down."
The man is suspected of three counts of assault and violently resisting an official.
Relief organisations are preparing to start working in Sulawesi, Indonesia - "Clean water is the most urgent"
UNICEF and other international aid agencies are ready to help on the island of Sulawesi in Indonesia, says Kirsi Haru, Head of Information at UNICEF Finland.
Local authorities will lead the rescue work, but the need for additional assistance is already obvious at this stage.
UNICEF operates in almost all countries, and we also have long-term programs in Indonesia.
In addition to normal operations, UNICEF always has full emergency aid readiness, says Haru.
The first task is now to find people in the ruins, both the survivings and the deceased.
Rescue operations are underway in the City of Palu in Sulawes, but slightly further north in the Donggala region, the situation is more unclear.
There are still places where the connections don't work.
Subsidy measures are difficult, because the roads are also cut off," Haru says.
At Sulawesi, buildings are often lightweight, so the damage caused by an earthquake and tsunami is great.
Once the survivors have been found, they'll need shelter at first, for example, tents and clean water.
In times of crisis, clean water is the most urgent.
As a first, it is usually possible to provide people with water purification tablets, which UNICEF also has in stock.
The aim is to repair the water systems as soon as possible, but in the event of an emergency, water is transported, for example, by trucks.
In times of crisis, many children are divorced from their parents.
Finding family members and combining families is one part of UNICEF's field of work.
Food aid, on the other hand, is organised, for example, by the World Food Programme (WFP).
In the early stages, previously collected funds will be used for emergency aid.
However, UNICEF, among others, has already started fundraising to help Indonesia's earthquake and tsunami victims.
Billionaire Elon Musk quits as Tesla's board of director - 20-million-dollar fine for tweets
Elon Musk, the founder of spacerockets and electric car companies, avoids deceission charges with the U.S. Securities Commission.
Elon Musk, a billionaire, will end his position as the chairman of Tesla's board of directors and agree to a 20-million-dollar fine.
Musk resigns as part of an agreement he entered into with the U.S. securities regulator.
By agreeing, Musk avoids fraud charges.
Musk was threatened by the U.S. Securities Commission (SEC) for securities fraud due to tweets he sent in August.
Musk wrote that he is preparing to withdraw the electric car company Tesla from the stock exchange, prompting the company's stock price to rise rapidly.
According to the agreement, Musk will not be allowed to serve as the chairman of Tesla's board for three years.
In addition, Tesla must appoint a new "independent member" and take two "independent leaders" into their management team.
Tesla must also set up a committee to monitor Musk's communications.
Musk will continue as Tesla's CEO.
In addition to Musk's personal fines of 20 million dollars, the Tesla company will also have to pay 20 million similar fines to a financial supervisor.
The tweets misled investors
According to the SEC, Musk's tweets misled investors and Tesla's shareholders.
Musk argued that he would have secured funding to buy Tesla back from the stock exchange as a private limited liability company.
According to the billionaire's tweets, the company would have been removed from the stock exchange at a share price of US$420.
Tesla's share price rose more than ten per century after tweets per day, but declined later.
According to the SEC, Muski's claims had no basis whatsoever.
According to the supervisor, he had not actually negotiated his claim about the funding.
He had also not discussed withdrawing from the stock exchange with another management team or the company's board of directors.
On Friday of this week, the rate dropped 14 per cent when the Securities Regulator SEC announced a fraud charge.
It is expected to reaffirm the company's exchange rate.
The final verdict isn't the reason
Naturally, the bells of the cathedral don't ring when the hands don't move.
It's a technical defect.
The bell of Helsinki Cathedral's tower has stopped.
The hands point to twelve o'clock.
It's like a mediocre horror movie: even when the church's crypta should be exorcised and the organ would ring on its own.
Naturally, this is not the case, but the clock has stopped due to a technical reason: it has broken.
It's been visited by a repairman, but some part has broken and we're waitinging for a new part on it," says shuntio Eveliina Jamsen.
At the same time, Jamsen confirms the point already mentioned in the Church Helsinki Twitter account.
The account mentions that the final judgment is not the reason for stopping the clock.
Jamsen says that church bells don't ring because of a fault.
